On a SS2 GX with 32/32MB and -DBLIT on it runs with no problem.  Nice and fast,
actually.  It looks like the code which keeps track of objects so they can
be erased (for non-blitting machines) keeps sizeable structs around (a
roughly 4k record for each of the 4k possible objects).  That's 16 Meg of
storage that can reclaimed by simply tracking only those objects that are
displayed.  If an object hasn't been drawn, you don't need to know where it
was in order to erase it, no? (A *triple* negative?  Eek.)  


